<?xml version="1.0" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE DAVEfunc PUBLIC "-//AIAA//DTD for Flight Dynamic Models - Functions 2.0//EN"  
          "http://www.daveml.org/DTDs/2p0/DAVEfunc.dtd">
<DAVEfunc>
  <!--

        These data are for the HL-20, a proposed lifting-body vehicle
        to be used for taking people to/from low-earth orbit. The
        simulation was used extensively in the 1990s at NASA's Langley
        Research Center for piloted studies of the approach and
        landing phase. The data is based on a variety of sources
        including wind-tunnel and computational fluid dynamics (CFD)
        calculations. The data was compiled by C. Cruz [1] and used in
        the studies conducted by Jackson.

        This file shows how the aerodynamics model can be captured
        using DAVE-ML for a realistic engineering-level vehicle.

        Created May 2003.

        Implementation notes:

        1) varIDs, gtIDs, etc. remain in FORTRAN variable naming
        scheme to better compare with documentation (TM-107580). So CL
        is coefficient of lift; CR is rolling moment coefficient, and
        CN is yawing moment (CY, CD, and CM are as expected).

        2) This is the first DAVE-ML file to use version 1.5 DTD, in
        which tables can be specified outside of functions and thus
        reused. So the wing- and body-flap contribution tables appear
        in pairs when referenced by the function definitions.

        3) This model is a polynomial buildup in powers of alpha
        (angle-of-attack). So there are four tables for each
        coefficient: a constant term, a term due to alpha, a term due
        to alpha^2 and an alpha^3 term. This is thus a fairly large
        example file!

  -->

  <!-- Notes from the C implementation (aero_v20.c, circa 911230)
       /******************************************************************************
       C
       C   TITLE:  AERO
       C
       C- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - 
       C   This code represent version 2.0 of the subsonic and low supersonic
       C   aero data for the HL-20 Lifting Body.  It was developed from a number
       C   of data sources and is valid over a Mach regime of 0 - 4.0, and
       C   for angles of attack as shown below: (approx)
       C
       C                                                maximum angle of attack
       C    30 |-                                     /vvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv
       C       |                                    / 
       C       |                                  /  
       C       |vvvvvvvvvvvvvvv|                /   
       C       |               |              /
       C       |               |            /
       C    20 |-              |          /
       C       |               |        /
       C       |               |      / 
       C       |               VVVVV/
       C       |
       C       |
       C    10 |-
       C       |
       C       |
       C       |
       C       |
       C       |
       C     0 |
       C       |^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
       C       |                 minimum angle of attack
       C       |
       C       |
       C       |
       C   -10 |_______________|_______________|_______________|_______________|
       C       0               1               2               3               4
       C                                   Mach number
       C       
       C
       C   FORCE AND MOMENT CONVENTIONS
       C
       C       Normal right-hand rule applies.  Forces are positive forward, right,
       C       and down.  Moments are positive according to right hand rule.
       C
       C   ANGULAR MEASUREMENT UNITS
       C
       C       Angle of attack (ALP) and sideslip (BETA) are in degrees.
       C       Body angular rates (PB, QB, RB) are in radians per second.
       C
       C   DEFLECTION CONVENTIONS
       C
       C       Wing Flaps, Body Flaps:         + => trailing edge down
       C       Rudder                          + => trailing edge left
       C
       C   COEFFICIENT NAMING CONVENTIONS
       C
       C       Lift:           CL
       C       Drag:           CD
       C       Pitch:          CM
       C       Sideforce:      CY
       C       Roll:           CR
       C       Yaw:            CN
       C               
       C       NOTE: Normal roll moment contributions, normally designated in
       C       aero models as 'Cl', or in Fortan sometimes as 'CLL' (for 'C' little
       C       'l'), are designated as 'CR' in this model, to avoid confusion
       C       with lift coefficients.
       C
       C- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - 
       C
       C   GENEALOGY:  Created by Bruce Jackson on 1991-04-24 from C. Cruz data
       C               Converted to ANSI C by Bruce Jackson on 1991-12-30
       C
       C- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - 
       C
       C   [Original C version] MODIFICATION HISTORY:
       C
       C   DATE          PURPOSE                                                    BY
       C   ====          =======                                                    ==
       C
       C   1991-04-24    Written from tabular data from Chris Cruz of
       C                 LaRC Vehicle Analysis Branch.                              EBJ
       C
       C   1991-05-16    Added common block COEFFS to pass intermediate
       C                 coefficient results; also corrected sign on
       C                 right hand surfaces for lat/dir coeffs                     EBJ
       C
       C   1991-06-06    Swapped coefficients for CLP & CNR to match
       C                 check plots from VAB.                                      EBJ
       C
       C   1991-12-30    Rewritten for C version                                    EBJ
       C
       C [ 2003-03-18    Converted to DAVE-ML format                                EBJ ]
       C
       C
  -->


  <!--                          =================                          -->
  <!-- =========================   FILE HEADER   ========================= -->
  <!--                          =================                          -->


  <fileHeader>
    <author name="Bruce Jackson" org="NASA Langley Research Center" 
            email="bruce.jackson@nasa.gov">
      <address>MS 132 NASA, Hampton, VA 23681</address>
    </author>
    <creationDate date="2003-03-18"/>

    <fileVersion>Mod K, 2010-12-01</fileVersion>

    <description>
      Version 2.0 aero model for HL-20 lifting body, as described in TM-107580 [01]. Original data
      built from wind tunnel test data described in [00]. This aero model was used for HL-20
      approach and landing studies at NASA Langley Research Center during 1989-1995 and for a
      follow-on study at NASA Johnson Space Center in 1994 and NASA Ames Research Center in
      2001. This DAVE-ML implementation created 2003 by Bruce Jackson to demonstrate DAVE-ML.
    </description>


    <reference refID="REF00"
	       author="Cruz, Christopher I.; Ware, George M.; Grafton, Sue B.; Woods, William C.; Young, James C."
	       title="Aerodynamic Characteristics of a Proposed Personnel Launch system (PLS) Lifting-Body Configuration at Mach Numbers from 0.05 to 20.3"
	       accession="NASA TM-101641"
	       date="1989-11-00"
               />

    <reference refID="REF01" 
               author="Jackson, E. Bruce; Cruz, Christopher I. &amp; and Ragsdale, W. A." 
               title="Real-Time Simulation Model of the HL-20 Lifting Body" 
               accession="NASA TM-107580"
               date="1992-07-01"
               />

